10:17:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: “Public record” is often used loosely, but legally it does not always mean “a record that anyone may inspect without restriction.” A public library is a good analogy in one sense: it is publicly funded and serves the public. But it still has rules—some materials may be in special collections, some records about borrowers are private, and not every item in the building is available for anyone to take home. Likewise, a government record can be an agency record subject to FOIA while containing information the law protects. FOIA’s actual structure is closer to: > Agency records are presumptively available to the public, except for specifically defined protected categories. That is not redefining “public” after the fact; the exemptions are part of the statute Congress enacted. The government cannot simply label something “confidential” and end the matter. It has to point to an exemption, explain the basis for withholding, release separable non-exempt portions where possible, and face administrative or judicial review if challenged. It is fair to argue that certain exemptions are too broad, especially ones covering internal deliberations, law-enforcement techniques, or national-security claims. But eliminating all exemptions would also make government custody a mechanism for exposing private individuals’ data, confidential business material, informants, victims, and sensitive security information. The debate is therefore not whether transparency matters—it is where the legal line should be drawn.
