10:17:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is the core risk, and FOIA has mechanisms meant to prevent it—though they do not always work perfectly in practice. Agencies cannot lawfully just stamp “internal deliberation” or “national security” on everything and end the inquiry: - **They must identify a legal basis.** Withholdings must fit one of FOIA’s nine exemptions, not merely an agency preference for secrecy. - **They must release segregable portions.** If only a paragraph, name, or attachment is exempt, the rest of the record generally must be released. - **They must explain themselves.** In litigation, agencies often must provide a detailed declaration or a *Vaughn index* describing what was withheld and which exemption applies, without revealing the protected content itself. - **Courts can review withholdings.** A federal judge can require more explanation, inspect records privately (*in camera*), and order disclosure. Courts review FOIA exemption claims de novo rather than simply deferring to an agency’s conclusion. - **Administrative appeals and mediation exist.** A requester can appeal within the agency and seek help from the Office of Government Information Services before suing. - **Congress, inspectors general, journalists, watchdogs, and the public can scrutinize patterns of over-redaction.** There are also limits specific to the examples you gave: - The “internal deliberation” exemption generally covers pre-decisional, deliberative communications—such as candid policy recommendations—not final agency policy, adopted reasoning, or purely factual material that can reasonably be separated. - The national-security exemption generally depends on information being properly classified under an authorized executive order. Courts may give substantial weight to agencies on genuine intelligence matters, but classification still has legal standards and procedures. Still, your concern is valid. Challenging a withholding can be slow, expensive, and difficult because the requester often cannot see what was withheld. Agencies sometimes overapply exemptions, and courts may be deferential in national-security cases. FOIA’s practical effectiveness depends heavily on requesters challenging improper secrecy, judges enforcing the statute, and Congress narrowing exemptions or improving enforcement when the balance has shifted too far toward secrecy.
